  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 225 PM EDT Sat Sep 5 2026\n\nOn the front edge of a ridge tonight, and back edge of upper trough \nover New England. Consequently, high pressure will be in control, \ndrifting across the Canadian Shield into the morning. Chance \nfor some late fog tonight, mainly across eastern upper \npotentially leaking down into Pellston and vicinity, and then \nacross the Interior near West Branch and vicinity. Overall \nshould be a brief concern during an otherwise calm and chilly \nnight. \n\nHigh pressure aloft continues to build over northern Michigan early \nnext week with raising heights, keeping the seasonably warm \nconditions around. At the same time, conglomerate of energy ejects \nout of the PacNW into the N Plains around Tuesday. Response will be \na sub 1000 mb low pressure system drawing an anomalous corridor of \nmoisture (+3.0 sigma PWs) northward into the Upper Midwest and Great \nLakes Region later-ish Tuesday into Wednesday.\n\nWarm, moist advection later Tuesday into Wednesday combined \nwith an approaching frontal boundary will lead to showers and \nembedded thunderstorms. Corridor of modest instability will aid \nin locally higher rainfall rates, especially given the anomalous\ncolumn moisture. That being said, current ENS guidance has a \nrelatively low probability for rain over 0.5\", highest probs \nfavoring the E UP. Nevertheless, widespread precip chances \nappear likely during this time frame. \n\nIn addition to the precipitation chances, combination of sfc high \npressure sliding off to the east and approaching low pressure mid \nweek will result in a tightening pressure gradient and increasing \nwinds aloft. Consequently, sfc winds will increase, with breezy \nsouthwest to west winds on Wednesday, with gusts 30+ mph currently \nin the forecast. \n\nCooler airmass filters in behind the mid week system, with Thursday \nbeing the coolest day. Few showers will be possible as well as a \nshort wave dives across N MI. Tranquil for at least a day or two\nbefore the next system/energy influences the region.",
